  <graph edgedefault="undirected">
    <node id="&quot;JOHN A. SEITZ&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PERSON"</data>
      <data key="d1">"John A. Seitz was a Brigadier General who commanded the 1st Infantry Division and the XVIII Airborne Corps. He was born in Leavenworth, Kansas, and graduated from the University of Kansas in 1932. He received the Mildred Clodfelter Alumni Award and had two wives, including Dorothy 'Dottie' Seitz."</data>
      <data key="d2">ramdocs-0491-0055941d9d4a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;RICHARD J. SEITZ&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PERSON"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Richard J. Seitz was a Lieutenant General and brother of Brigadier General John A. Seitz. He graduated from Leavenworth High School and served as a paratrooper during World War II, commanding the 2nd Battalion, 517th Parachute Infantry Regiment, the 82nd Airborne Division, and the XVIII Airborne Corps."</data>
      <data key="d2">ramdocs-0491-0055941d9d4a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;LEAVENWORTH, KANSAS&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"LOCATION"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Leavenworth, Kansas is the birthplace of Brigadier General John A. Seitz and the location where his brother, Lieutenant General Richard J. Seitz, graduated from Leavenworth High School."</data>
      <data key="d2">ramdocs-0491-0055941d9d4a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;1ST INFANTRY DIVISION&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"ORGANIZATION"</data>
      <data key="d1">"The 1st Infantry Division is a military unit that participated in the Battle of the Bulge and was off the front line near Aubel, Belgium."&lt;SEP&gt;"The 1st Infantry Division is an organization commanded by Brigadier General John A. Seitz."</data>
      <data key="d2">ramdocs-0491-0055941d9d4a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;XVIII AIRBORNE CORPS&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"ORGANIZATION"</data>
      <data key="d1">"The XVIII Airborne Corps is an organization commanded by Brigadier General John A. Seitz."</data>
      <data key="d2">ramdocs-0491-0055941d9d4a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;DOROTHY 'DOTTIE' SEITZ&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PERSON"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Dorothy 'Dottie' Seitz was the second wife of Brigadier General John A. Seitz and was previously married to Colonel Seaborn."</data>
      <data key="d2">ramdocs-0491-0055941d9d4a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;ROYCE&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PERSON"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Royce died from cancer, indicating a personal health issue."&lt;SEP&gt;"Royce is mentioned as a family member who died from cancer in early 1972."</data>
      <data key="d2">ramdocs-0491-0055941d9d4a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;MAJOR GENERAL HUEBNER&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PERSON"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Huebner became the deputy commander of V Corps and later the commander, showcasing his progression in the military hierarchy."&lt;SEP&gt;"Major General Huebner became deputy commander of V Corps and later commander when Lieutenant General Gerow became commander of the new 15th United States Army."&lt;SEP&gt;"Major General Huebner became the deputy commander of V Corps and later the commander, showcasing his progression in the military hierarchy."</data>
      <data key="d2">ramdocs-0491-0055941d9d4a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;V CORPS&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"ORGANIZATION"</data>
      <data key="d1">"V Corps is a military unit that had a deputy commander, Major General Huebner, and later a commander, Major General Huebner, after Lieutenant General Gerow took over."&lt;SEP&gt;"V Corps is an organization where Major General Huebner served as deputy commander."</data>
      <data key="d2">ramdocs-0491-0055941d9d4a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;ARLINGTON NATIONAL CEMETERY&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"LOCATION"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Arlington National Cemetery is the burial site of Lieutenant Colonel John F. R. 'Jeff' Seitz."&lt;SEP&gt;"Arlington National Cemetery is the final resting place of Major General John F. R. 'Jeff' Seitz, indicating a significant historical site."&lt;SEP&gt;"Arlington National Cemetery is the final resting place of Major General John F. R. 'Jeff' Seitz."</data>
      <data key="d2">ramdocs-0491-0055941d9d4a</data>
    </node>
